               <physDesc>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <binding contemporary="true">
                        <p>Medieval binding; bookmarks on front cover (corners and centre); flaps
                           project above headbands.</p>
                     </binding>
                  </bindingDesc>
               </physDesc>
               <history>
                  <origin> <origDate when-custom="118X/119X" notBefore="1180" notAfter="1199"
                     calendar="#gregorian">s. xii ex</origDate> </origin>
                  <provenance type="#b s" cert="low" source="552"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_83"
                     >Buildwas, Shropshire. Savigniac and (1147) Cistercian abbey of St Mary the
                     Virgin and St Chad</orgName><p>Medieval binding, with nailmarks for bosses at
                     each corner and in centre of front cover (cf. Bodley 371, 395); style of
                     initials (cf. Bodley 395) suggests provenance; inscription in alternate green
                     and black words 'Liber sancte marie [. . . .]' at foot of verso of flyleaf
                     facing first leaf of text in a late s. xii hand, same as that of table of
                     contents on the same page above.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#e" source="3837"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_340">London.
                     Carmelite convent</orgName><p>Later belonged to the London Carmelites.
                     <q>Conventus Carmelitorum Londonie ex assignacione fratris Thome Walden.</q>
                     (fol. 1r, at top). This is Thomas Netter of Walden (1370–1440), prior
                     provincial of the Carmelite order in England. Netter also gave Oxford, Trinity
                     College, MS 58, and Cambridge, St John's I.15. Leland (Collectanea 4. 52) says
                     that he gave many more.</p><p>I 66m (fol. 1r, at top, above right side of 1st
                     col.).</p><q type="pressmark">I.66m</q></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_458">Reading, Berkshire,
                     Benedictine abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin</orgName> <p>This attribution was
                     rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964),
                     which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval
                     Libraries of Great Britain index card originally offered the following
                     interpretation of the erased inscription: <q>Liber sancte Marie <supplied>de
                     Radyngia</supplied></q>. However, the supplied phrase 'de Radyngia' was later
                     crossed out, and a note added above: ?Buildwas.</p></provenance>
               </history>
